## 4. Technology & solution architecture

Architecture. A weekly batch analytics pipeline with a thin delivery layer, designed to run cheaply on State infrastructure and to be maintainable by AMIU staff.

Data flow
1. Ingest: AMIU daily prices and arrivals (API, database export or spreadsheet upload, as AMIU prefers) and scheduled pulls from the data.gov.in Open Government Data API (Agmarknet prices for Assam and comparable States). Optional: IMD rainfall, active futures prices, cold-storage stocks.
2. Validate and harmonise: market, commodity, variety and unit mapping; outlier screening against each series' recent median; explicit missing-week flags. Records are never silently changed; corrections are versioned with user and reason.
3. Weekly series: daily records become weekly median prices per market, commodity and variety, keeping gaps explicit.
4. Features: price changes over 1 to 52 weeks, volatility, reporting completeness, regional price indices built from comparable markets, the market's price relative to its peers, seasonality and, once AMIU data are connected, arrivals.
5. Models and rules (below), then the decision layer.
6. Review: AMIU approves the weekly batch in a web console.
7. Delivery: SMS, WhatsApp, IVR and AEA dashboard; feedback stored for evaluation.

Models and methods
- Short horizon: a global gradient-boosted tree model (scikit-learn HistGradientBoostingRegressor) trained with quantile loss to predict one-to-four-week log price changes at the 10th, 50th and 90th percentiles. It handles missing values natively, so incomplete histories need no imputation, and one model shared across markets lets data-poor markets borrow strength from data-rich ones.
- Benchmarks: no-change and same-weeks-last-year (seasonal naive). Every series is scored weekly with rolling-origin, out-of-sample evaluation; publication is gated on beating the benchmarks.
- Calibration: band coverage is tracked; bands are widened (conformal adjustment) where they prove too narrow.
- Long horizon: seasonal price profiles pooled across Assam and neighbouring-State markets, with year-to-year ranges; later combined with cost and yield data into margin-and-risk rankings.
- Decision rules: transparent, versioned rules (expected gain after storage costs and losses versus downside risk), configurable per commodity by AMIU.

Stack
- Python 3.13, polars (lazy queries over Parquet), scikit-learn, marimo notebooks for reproducible, auditable analysis, seaborn for charts; uv for reproducible environments. All open source.
- Production: PostgreSQL for records and audit logs, Parquet for analytical history, a small REST API (FastAPI) for the dashboard, partner systems and channel gateways, and a mobile-friendly web dashboard in Assamese and English.
- Integrations: data.gov.in OGD API (working in the prototype); AMIU data feed (to be agreed); SMS via a DLT-registered gateway; WhatsApp Business Platform; an IVR provider; Bhashini services for Assamese translation and speech, subject to evaluation.

Hosting. Assam State Data Centre or a MeitY-empanelled cloud region in India. Compute needs are modest: the prototype's complete pipeline, including a year-long backtest with ${n_refits} model refits, runs in about ${runtime_min} minutes on a laptop.

## 5. Development methodology & intermediate steps

Evidence first, in short iterations. Every stage ends with an artefact the department can inspect, and a model is promoted only when it beats simple benchmarks out of sample.

1. Discovery and requirements (weeks 1–4)
- Workshops with the Agriculture Department, AMIU and AAU; interviews with AEAs and farmers in pilot districts.
- Agree pilot commodities, markets and districts, the decisions to support (sell, hold, store, crop choice) and success measures.
- Output: requirements and pilot-scope note; data-sharing request to AMIU.

2. Data audit and pipeline (weeks 3–8)
- Profile AMIU and Agmarknet data: coverage, gaps, units, varieties, renamed markets, outliers.
- Build ingestion, harmonisation and quality checks; set up data-quality alerts for AEAs.
- Output: data audit report and reproducible pipeline.

3. Design (weeks 5–10)
- Co-design advisory formats with AEAs and farmers: SMS, WhatsApp card, voice script, dashboard, printable sheet.
- Draft Assamese content; native-speaker review; comprehension tests with small farmer groups.
- Output: validated wireframes and message templates.

4. Prototype (done before submission)
- On public Agmarknet data: harmonised weekly series, global quantile model, benchmarks, rolling-origin backtest, incomplete-data stress test, decision rules, seasonal crop cards and mock-ups.
- Output: prototype code, backtest report and Proof of Work (attached).

5. MVP (months 3–5)
- Retrain on AMIU data and add arrivals features; calibrate decision rules with AAU storage-cost and loss parameters; build AMIU review console, AEA dashboard and SMS, WhatsApp and IVR delivery.
- Output: MVP running end to end on pilot scope.

6. Testing (months 4–6)
- Technical: automated tests of data rules, leakage checks and reproducibility checks.
- Statistical: weekly rolling-origin evaluation against no-change and seasonal-naive benchmarks; band calibration.
- Shadow mode: forecasts produced and scored for six to eight weeks without being sent to farmers.
- Usability: comprehension tests of Assamese messages; AEA feedback.
- Output: test report and validation-readiness review with the department.

7. Pilot validation and refinement (months 7–12)
- One crop season with continuous monitoring, a mid-pilot review and updates to rules and content from feedback.
- Output: pilot evaluation report and scale-up recommendation.

Principles
- Benchmarks first: a model is used only where it beats no-change and seasonal-naive forecasts.
- Human in the loop: AMIU approves every advisory batch.
- Reproducibility: every number in a report is generated by versioned code from versioned data (already the case in this proposal).
- Open, documented code to enable handover to AMIU.

## 6. Development timeline & milestones

Indicative timeline from project start (month 0). The public-data prototype is already complete.

Phase 1 — Discovery and data access (month 1)
- Deliverables: pilot scope note (commodities, markets, districts), requirements, data-sharing request, risk register.
- Milestone M1: pilot scope signed off by the department.
- Dependencies: a nominated department counterpart; AMIU and AAU availability.

Phase 2 — Data foundation (months 1–2)
- Deliverables: data audit report on AMIU and Agmarknet data; ingestion and harmonisation pipeline; data-quality dashboard.
- Milestone M2: historical AMIU data loaded and profiled.
- Dependencies: receipt of AMIU historical data and agreement on the daily feed format.

Phase 3 — Forecast engine v1 (months 2–3)
- Deliverables: global quantile models for pilot commodities retrained on AMIU and public data; backtest report against benchmarks; seasonal crop cards.
- Milestone M3: backtest report reviewed by AMIU and AAU experts.

Phase 4 — Decision layer and content (months 3–4)
- Deliverables: Hold / Sell / Store rules calibrated with AAU storage-cost and loss parameters; Assamese message templates reviewed by native speakers; comprehension-test results.
- Milestone M4: advisory formats approved.
- Dependencies: AAU post-harvest parameters; a native-speaker reviewer.

Phase 5 — Delivery MVP (months 4–5)
- Deliverables: AMIU review console, AEA dashboard, SMS (DLT-registered templates), WhatsApp and IVR integration; security review.
- Milestone M5: end-to-end dry run on pilot scope.
- Dependencies: DLT sender ID and template registration, WhatsApp Business verification and the hosting environment, all started in month 1.

Phase 6 — Shadow mode and validation readiness (months 5–6)
- Deliverables: six to eight weeks of shadow forecasts scored against actual prices; test report; user and operations manuals; AEA training.
- Milestone M6 (end of month 6): validation-readiness review. The solution is ready for pilot validation.

Phase 7 — Pilot validation (months 7–12; one crop season of four to six months)
- Live advisories to registered farmers in pilot districts; weekly scorecards; mid-pilot review; final evaluation report with a scale-up plan.
- Pilot timing will follow the crop calendar of the chosen commodities, agreed in Phase 1.

Critical path: AMIU data access (M2), then model retraining (M3), content approval (M4) and channel registration (M5). Channel registration and hosting run in parallel from month 1, and development continues on public data if AMIU data arrive late, so a delay in data access slows retraining but does not stop the project.

## 7. Data & information requirements

Principle: the prototype uses only public data. No access to departmental data is assumed; every non-public item below is a request.

A. Used in the prototype (public, confirmed)
- Agmarknet daily market prices through the data.gov.in OGD API: resource "Variety-wise Daily Market Prices Data of Commodity" (Directorate of Marketing & Inspection), Government Open Data License – India. Fields: date, state, district, market, commodity, variety, grade, minimum, maximum and modal price (₹ per quintal).
- Retrieved ${retrieved}: ${rows_total} rows, covering all Assam commodities from ${assam_first_year} and the three pilot commodities in ${n_comp_states} neighbouring States from 2016.
- This public dataset has no arrivals field, so arrivals are not yet used.

B. Requested from AMIU / AAU for development and testing
- Historical daily prices and arrivals collected by AEAs, all available years, with market and commodity codes (CSV, Excel or database export).
- The daily feed going forward (API, shared database or scheduled export) and its timing.
- Market master (name variants, location, market type, reporting AEA) and the commodity, variety, grade and unit codebook.
- Known data issues, such as non-reporting periods or unit changes.

C. Requested from AAU and the Department for decision rules and crop planning
- Storage costs and practices by crop, post-harvest loss rates and shelf life.
- Crop calendars by agro-climatic zone; cost of cultivation and yields for candidate crops; district area and production statistics.

D. Supplementary public sources, to be assessed
- Comparable markets in neighbouring States via the same OGD API (already used).
- IMD rainfall; commodity futures prices only where trading is active (SEBI suspended futures in several agricultural commodities, including rapeseed-mustard, from December 2021, so availability will be verified per commodity); cold-storage stock reports; festival calendar.

E. Collected during the pilot, with informed consent
- Farmer registration: mobile number, language, village, crops, preferred market; the minimum needed.
- Feedback: price received, date and market; usefulness ratings.

Formats and processing
- Inputs: CSV, Excel, JSON (API); Parquet internally.
- Processing: name and unit harmonisation, de-duplication, outlier screening, weekly aggregation with explicit gaps. In the prototype, ${outlier_pct}% of weekly prices were flagged as implausible jumps (more than threefold against recent weeks) and excluded.
- Annotation: AMIU analysts label suspected data errors and add event notes (floods, strikes, policy changes) in the review console. These labels improve both the quality rules and the model.

Dependencies and assumptions
- AMIU data access depends on a data-sharing agreement. If it is delayed, development continues on public data, which already works.
- Arrivals data are assumed to exist in AMIU records, as the challenge states, but their format and history length are unknown.
- Personal data are not needed to build or test models; only aggregated market data are.

